  "query_preview": "..\nRest of tonight through Friday night: The Thanksgiving Atmospheric \nRiver is coming to an end across WA and the ID Panhandle. A cold \nfront has shifted the Pacific moisture plume to the south \nacross OR into central ID. Another shortwave will quickly push \nacross the Northwest Friday afternoon into Friday evening, \nexcept the bulk of the dynamics with this next shortwave will \nmove into OR. A warm front will begin to push north into \nsouthern WA, but model guidance is consistent that the \nprecipitation band with this front will only progress as far \nnorth as about the Highway 2 corridor and that delineating line\ncarries a 50% chance for measurable precipitation. Total \nprecipitation for Wenatchee to Moses Lake to Ritzville is \nexpected to around a tenth of an inch. Better upslope flow into \nthe Palouse, Central Panhandle Mountains, and Camas Prairie will\nresult in between a quarter and half of an inch of \nprecipitation. Snow levels will be between 4,000 and 5,000 feet,\nbut they do come down on the backside of the precipitation with\ncold air advection Friday night. This may result in a brief \nchance over to snow over the Camas Prairie.\n\nSaturday through Monday night: A break between weather systems with \ndrier weather comes on Saturday. This will be the best travel day \nwithout any threat of precipitation. That changes on Sunday and \nthere is some uncertainty with the timing of precipitation. Quicker \nmodels has precipitation moving in with a warm front by as early as \nSunday morning. This seems to be too early as the trend is for a \nlater timing in the afternoon to evening with latest runs of the \nmodel ensembles. Snow levels will start out around 3,000 to 4,000 \nSunday and Sunday night then rise on Monday. Expect light snow over \nthe mountain passes with the potential for minor travel impacts. The \nthreat for precipitation will continue into the first part of Monday \nbefore winding down in the afternoon into the evening. Winds will \npick up from the southwest, but will just be generally a little \nbreezy with gusts up to around 25-35 mph expected across the exposed \nareas of the Columbia Basin.\n\nTuesday through Thursday: A ridge of high pressure builds in Tuesday \ninto Wednesday with a couple of dry days. Temperatures cool slightly \nto around normal. Expect the potential for fog in the valleys. Then \nmodel guidance indicates a potential for another weather disturbance \naround Thursday. Early indications is that this weather feature will \nbe a quick hitting system with light precipitaiton amounts. There is \na potential for a mix of rain and snow in the mountain valleys, \nespecially if this weather system swings through more so in the \nmorning hours on Thursday. /SVH",
